# Kelola — design system rules for Cursor

These rules are not suggestions. If a change violates one, stop and
use the existing component instead of inventing a new pattern.

## Source of truth

`lib/design/kelola_theme.dart` and `lib/design/kelola_components.dart`
are the ONLY source of visual truth. `DESIGN.html` and any screenshots
in `/design-reference/` are references for what these components
should look like once composed — they are not themselves the
implementation target.

Before building ANY screen or widget:
1. Read `lib/design/kelola_theme.dart` and `lib/design/kelola_components.dart` in full.
2. Check whether `RiskBand`, `StatCard`, `ServiceRow`, `Sparkline`,
   or `DestructiveConfirmSheet` already covers this UI need.
3. If it does, compose with it. Do not rebuild an equivalent.
4. If it genuinely doesn't, propose the new component as an addition
   to `kelola_components.dart` — do not inline a one-off widget in a
   screen file.

## Hard bans

These patterns must NEVER appear anywhere in this codebase, even if
they seem like a reasonable interpretation of "dashboard" or
"system stats":

- Radial gauges / dial / speedometer widgets, for ANY metric
  (CPU, memory, disk, load). Use `StatCard` + `Sparkline` only.
- Floating action buttons (FAB). Actions live inline in the
  relevant screen, not in a floating global button.
- Plain `Card`, `ListTile`, or Material default list rows for
  anything representing a host, service, container, or action.
  Use `RiskBand` / `ServiceRow`.
- `AlertDialog` for any `RiskLevel.destructive` action. Use
  `DestructiveConfirmSheet`.
- Any hardcoded `Color(0x...)` outside `kelola_theme.dart`. Always
  go through `context.kc.<token>` or `context.kc.forRisk(risk)`.
- Any font family other than SpaceGrotesk / IBMPlexSans / IBMPlexMono.
- Green used for anything other than "healthy". Red used for
  anything other than destructive/error. Amber is the only
  decorative accent color in the app — do not introduce a second
  accent color for visual variety.
- Bottom sheets must always wrap their body in `KelolaSheet` (from
  `kelola_components.dart`). Do not call `showModalBottomSheet` with
  a raw column that only pads `viewInsets` or a fixed bottom number.
- List/grid bottom padding must use `kelolaScrollPadding(...)` (or
  `MediaQuery.viewPadding.bottom + extra`). Never hardcode 16/24/28/32
  as the sole bottom inset for scrollable content.

## The type rule

If a string came from the server (a command, a log line, a PID, a
path, an exit code, a timestamp) → `KelolaType.mono`.
If a human or Kelola wrote the string (a screen title, a button
label, a description) → `KelolaType.display` or `KelolaType.body`.
Never mix these up for "visual balance."

## Workflow

- Build `lib/design/style_guide_screen.dart` FIRST — a single screen
  rendering every component in `kelola_components.dart` in isolation
  with sample data. Reference it constantly.
- After building each real screen, compare it side-by-side against
  the matching frame in `DESIGN.html` before moving to the next
  screen. Do not batch multiple screens before review.
- If a screen doesn't match, fix it before proceeding — do not
  accumulate drift across screens.

## Dark theme only

Do not build or scaffold a light theme in v1. `buildKelolaDarkTheme()`
is the only theme. If asked to add light mode, decline and note it's
deferred per spec.
